1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,322
In nineteen seventy four, Canada walked out of a half built nuclear reactor on the Chambal river, in Rajasthan.

2
00:00:07,322 --> 00:00:08,893
India finished it.

3
00:00:08,893 --> 00:00:12,386
But twenty years later, its heart was wearing out.

4
00:00:12,386 --> 00:00:15,325
Three hundred and six thin metal tubes.

5
00:00:15,325 --> 00:00:18,596
Only one country had ever replaced tubes like these.

6
00:00:18,596 --> 00:00:19,506
Canada.

7
00:00:19,506 --> 00:00:23,302
And Canada's export rules kept its companies from helping.

8
00:00:23,302 --> 00:00:28,240
So how do you rebuild the heart of a machine that nobody will teach you to open?

9
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:34,677
By the end, you will see Indian engineers pull those tubes out, one by one, into a steel flask.

10
00:00:34,677 --> 00:00:41,476
And you will see why it matters for the new reactor that began taking in fuel this September, on the same lake.

11
00:00:41,476 --> 00:00:43,537
Let me take you to Rawatbhata.

12
00:00:43,537 --> 00:00:48,835
A small town on the Rana Pratap Sagar lake, about sixty four kilometres from Kota.

13
00:00:48,835 --> 00:00:55,637
If you had come here in the nineteen sixties, you would have seen Canada and India building a nuclear power station together.

14
00:00:55,637 --> 00:00:57,673
Its design came from Canada.

15
00:00:57,673 --> 00:00:59,153
And why that design?

16
00:00:59,153 --> 00:01:01,801
India had picked it for one simple reason.

17
00:01:01,801 --> 00:01:04,122
It needs no enriched uranium.

18
00:01:04,122 --> 00:01:09,012
You can run it on natural uranium, with the same mix of atoms you find in nature.

19
00:01:09,012 --> 00:01:11,523
And Indian factories could build it.

20
00:01:11,523 --> 00:01:16,215
Now, you have probably seen a reactor drawn as one giant steel pot.

21
00:01:16,215 --> 00:01:18,684
Most reactors in the world are just that.

22
00:01:18,684 --> 00:01:20,655
This one is different.

23
00:01:20,655 --> 00:01:24,402
Imagine a tank, pierced by three hundred and six tubes.

24
00:01:24,402 --> 00:01:28,095
Each tube, if you like, is its own small pressure cooker.

25
00:01:28,095 --> 00:01:29,799
Think of the cooker in your kitchen.

26
00:01:29,799 --> 00:01:32,590
You know that whistle, you have heard it all your life.

27
00:01:32,590 --> 00:01:37,409
Inside, the steam pushes at about one kilo on every square centimetre.

28
00:01:37,409 --> 00:01:40,799
Now jump ahead to India's newest reactors for a moment.

29
00:01:40,799 --> 00:01:47,558
There, the water in each tube is pushed a hundred times harder, at three hundred and ten degrees.

30
00:01:47,558 --> 00:01:53,229
And in reactors of this family, the wall of that tube is only about four millimetres thick.

31
00:01:53,229 --> 00:01:54,413
Think of a pencil.

32
00:01:54,413 --> 00:01:56,482
The wall is thinner than that.

33
00:01:56,482 --> 00:01:58,953
Then came May nineteen seventy four.

34
00:01:58,953 --> 00:02:00,002
You remember it.

35
00:02:00,002 --> 00:02:01,049
Pokhran.

36
00:02:01,049 --> 00:02:03,075
Why did Canada care so much?

37
00:02:03,075 --> 00:02:05,360
India called its test peaceful.

38
00:02:05,360 --> 00:02:12,026
Canada saw plutonium from a research reactor near Mumbai, called Sigh-rus, which Canada had helped design.

39
00:02:12,026 --> 00:02:14,365
So Canada stopped its nuclear help.

40
00:02:14,365 --> 00:02:16,706
The engineers packed up and went home.

41
00:02:16,706 --> 00:02:18,643
The first unit was running.

42
00:02:18,643 --> 00:02:24,925
The second, as an Indian magazine put it in nineteen ninety six, the Canadians abandoned halfway.

43
00:02:24,925 --> 00:02:29,499
Now you might ask me, Kishore, so what happened to your half built reactor?

44
00:02:29,499 --> 00:02:32,083
Indian engineers finished it on their own.

45
00:02:32,083 --> 00:02:34,350
It went to work in nineteen eighty one.

46
00:02:34,350 --> 00:02:36,296
And for a while, all was well.

47
00:02:36,296 --> 00:02:44,211
At Kaiga in Karnataka, a reactor of this family once ran for nine hundred and sixty two days without stopping.

48
00:02:44,211 --> 00:02:45,439
A world record.

49
00:02:45,439 --> 00:02:47,934
So what stops a machine like that?

50
00:02:47,934 --> 00:02:49,801
You might guess the fuel.

51
00:02:49,801 --> 00:02:51,182
It is not the fuel.

52
00:02:51,182 --> 00:02:53,889
You can feed fresh fuel in while it runs.

53
00:02:53,889 --> 00:02:56,083
What wears out is the tubes.

54
00:02:56,083 --> 00:02:59,081
The early tubes were made of an older metal mix.

55
00:02:59,081 --> 00:03:01,627
Over the years they soaked up hydrogen.

56
00:03:01,627 --> 00:03:08,516
N-P-C-I-L, the company that runs India's nuclear plants, says they lasted only ten to twelve years.

57
00:03:08,516 --> 00:03:13,465
In August nineteen eighty three, near Toronto, the world saw what that means.

58
00:03:13,465 --> 00:03:17,198
At the Pickering station, one tube broke at full power.

59
00:03:17,198 --> 00:03:19,402
Just one, out of hundreds.

60
00:03:19,402 --> 00:03:21,316
But that is all it takes.

61
00:03:21,316 --> 00:03:23,376
How does a tube just break?

62
00:03:23,376 --> 00:03:26,234
Each hot tube sits inside a cooler tube.

63
00:03:26,234 --> 00:03:30,077
Small coiled springs, wrapped around it, keep the two apart.

64
00:03:30,077 --> 00:03:33,463
In that one channel, a spring had slipped out of place.

65
00:03:33,463 --> 00:03:35,752
Slowly, the hot tube sagged.

66
00:03:35,752 --> 00:03:37,874
Then it touched the cold one.

67
00:03:37,874 --> 00:03:40,731
Now think of a glass of cold water on a hot day.

68
00:03:40,731 --> 00:03:44,278
You have seen the drops gather on the outside, on the cold glass.

69
00:03:44,278 --> 00:03:48,484
Inside the metal, hydrogen did something like the drops you just pictured.

70
00:03:48,484 --> 00:03:50,650
It crept to the coldest spot.

71
00:03:50,650 --> 00:03:53,861
And there it hardened into brittle blisters.

72
00:03:53,861 --> 00:03:56,151
The blisters cracked.

73
00:03:56,151 --> 00:03:59,039
And the crack kept growing, until the tube split.

74
00:03:59,039 --> 00:04:03,086
The operators, I should tell you, brought the reactor safely to a stop.

75
00:04:03,086 --> 00:04:06,912
But every reactor of this kind now had a question to answer.

76
00:04:06,912 --> 00:04:10,713
India's tubes at Rawatbhata were made of the same older metal.

77
00:04:10,713 --> 00:04:17,742
And as Business India reported, Indian engineers were denied detailed knowledge of the accident, its cause and the remedy.

78
00:04:17,742 --> 00:04:19,977
So India had the same disease.

79
00:04:19,977 --> 00:04:21,562
And no doctor would come.

80
00:04:21,562 --> 00:04:22,852
What would you have done?

81
00:04:23,560 --> 00:04:28,174
In nineteen ninety five, a nuclear trade weekly reported India's decision.

82
00:04:28,174 --> 00:04:33,360
Rajasthan unit two would get all new tubes, with or without outside help.

83
00:04:33,360 --> 00:04:38,959
India was still a member of the Kan-doo Owners Group, the club of countries that run this kind of reactor.

84
00:04:38,959 --> 00:04:44,586
But Canada's export rules kept Canadian firms from supplying the tools, or doing the job.

85
00:04:44,586 --> 00:04:46,410
So the job came home.

86
00:04:46,410 --> 00:04:49,838
The project director at Rawatbhata was V K Chaturvedi.

87
00:04:49,838 --> 00:04:53,621
The team that would change the tubes was led by R C Arya.

88
00:04:53,621 --> 00:04:55,493
Can you picture the hardest part?

89
00:04:55,493 --> 00:05:00,986
Every one of the three hundred and six tubes had been inside a running reactor for years.

90
00:05:00,986 --> 00:05:02,588
You cannot stand near it.

91
00:05:02,588 --> 00:05:03,921
Not for a minute.

92
00:05:03,921 --> 00:05:09,003
So every cut, every pull, every weld had to be done by machines.

93
00:05:09,003 --> 00:05:11,522
Machines that did not exist yet.

94
00:05:11,522 --> 00:05:13,427
Indian engineers designed them.

95
00:05:13,427 --> 00:05:15,704
Now picture one old tube with me.

96
00:05:15,704 --> 00:05:18,105
A tool reaches in and cuts it free.

97
00:05:18,105 --> 00:05:22,170
The tube is drawn, slowly, into a heavy steel flask.

98
00:05:22,170 --> 00:05:24,668
The flask carries it away to a vault.

99
00:05:24,668 --> 00:05:27,428
You will not see a single person beside it.

100
00:05:27,428 --> 00:05:29,565
Everything is done from a distance.

101
00:05:29,565 --> 00:05:32,515
Then, as you watch, a new tube goes in.

102
00:05:32,515 --> 00:05:36,551
This time, made of a stronger mix, zirconium with niobium.

103
00:05:36,551 --> 00:05:37,695
And then the next.

104
00:05:37,695 --> 00:05:39,914
Three hundred and six times.

105
00:05:39,914 --> 00:05:41,404
And what did all this cost?

106
00:05:41,404 --> 00:05:48,637
Going by Business India's figures, turned into rupees at that year's rate, India planned to spend about two hundred and fifty crore.

107
00:05:48,637 --> 00:05:52,628
Canada had spent more than a thousand crore on the same kind of job.

108
00:05:52,628 --> 00:05:53,541
Was it quick?

109
00:05:53,541 --> 00:05:54,141
No.

110
00:05:54,141 --> 00:05:59,039
The reactor made no power in nineteen ninety five, ninety six or ninety seven.

111
00:05:59,039 --> 00:06:04,532
By nineteen ninety eight, if you drove past Rawatbhata, you would have seen unit two running again.

112
00:06:04,532 --> 00:06:09,836
N-P-C-I-L calls it the first such job in India, and only the second in the world.

113
00:06:09,836 --> 00:06:12,020
The first was in Canada itself.

114
00:06:12,020 --> 00:06:16,577
Bhabha Atomic Research Centre later counted what tools like these gave back.

115
00:06:16,577 --> 00:06:20,695
About one thousand one hundred crore rupees of extra electricity.

116
00:06:20,695 --> 00:06:23,612
You remember how we never threw away a broken radio?

117
00:06:23,612 --> 00:06:24,771
We opened it.

118
00:06:24,771 --> 00:06:26,910
That is the lesson of Rawatbhata.

119
00:06:26,910 --> 00:06:31,352
A machine is truly yours only when you can open it, and mend it.

120
00:06:31,352 --> 00:06:36,467
If you want the next machine opened like this, subscribe, so the next one comes to you.

121
00:06:36,467 --> 00:06:37,401
On the T-V?

122
00:06:37,401 --> 00:06:39,362
Just press Subscribe with your remote.

123
00:06:39,362 --> 00:06:40,937
But I must be fair with you.

124
00:06:40,937 --> 00:06:44,240
You may have heard people say these tubes are dangerous.

125
00:06:44,240 --> 00:06:46,005
Is it safe to live near one?

126
00:06:46,005 --> 00:06:47,974
Let me show you the worst day.

127
00:06:47,974 --> 00:06:50,303
The eleventh of March, twenty sixteen.

128
00:06:50,303 --> 00:06:51,939
Kakrapar, in Gujarat.

129
00:06:51,939 --> 00:06:55,000
At about nine in the morning, a tube began to leak.

130
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:58,251
The reactor shut itself down, the way you would want it to.

131
00:06:58,251 --> 00:06:59,994
There was no fuel failure.

132
00:06:59,994 --> 00:07:06,564
The regulator said there was no radiation harm to the workers, to the people outside, or to the land around it.

133
00:07:06,564 --> 00:07:08,586
So how serious was it?

134
00:07:08,586 --> 00:07:12,132
The international scale rated it two, out of seven.

135
00:07:12,132 --> 00:07:14,894
An incident, not an accident.

136
00:07:14,894 --> 00:07:16,067
What went wrong?

137
00:07:16,067 --> 00:07:19,285
A wrong chemical had got into the gas around the tubes.

138
00:07:19,285 --> 00:07:22,313
It had eaten small spots into their outer walls.

139
00:07:22,313 --> 00:07:24,065
And was it everywhere?

140
00:07:24,065 --> 00:07:24,719
No.

141
00:07:24,719 --> 00:07:28,110
The regulator found it only in Kakrapar's two units.

142
00:07:28,110 --> 00:07:32,545
So, as you might expect, Kakrapar did what Rawatbhata had taught.

143
00:07:32,545 --> 00:07:34,133
Every tube came out.

144
00:07:34,133 --> 00:07:36,551
Three hundred and six in each reactor.

145
00:07:36,551 --> 00:07:40,734
A former chief of N-P-C-I-L put it in words you will not forget.

146
00:07:40,734 --> 00:07:44,339
Replacing them is like a heart transplant for a human being.

147
00:07:44,339 --> 00:07:47,283
And that was the start of a bigger story.

148
00:07:47,283 --> 00:07:50,778
Now look at the reactor face with me, and count with your eyes.

149
00:07:50,778 --> 00:07:54,509
Three hundred and six circles grow to three hundred and ninety two.

150
00:07:54,509 --> 00:07:59,314
This is India's own seven hundred megawatt reactor, designed here.

151
00:07:59,314 --> 00:08:01,984
Around each tube flows a dry gas.

152
00:08:01,984 --> 00:08:07,696
If even a drop of water leaks in, the gas turns damp, like the dew on your cold glass.

153
00:08:07,696 --> 00:08:08,901
Remember that glass?

154
00:08:08,901 --> 00:08:11,257
Here, the dew is the alarm.

155
00:08:11,257 --> 00:08:15,587
The first one, at Kakrapar, went critical in July twenty twenty.

156
00:08:15,587 --> 00:08:16,389
Critical?

157
00:08:16,389 --> 00:08:17,736
That is good news here.

158
00:08:17,736 --> 00:08:21,260
It means the chain reaction has started, and holds steady.

159
00:08:21,260 --> 00:08:23,923
Many of its tests were done during the lockdown.

160
00:08:23,923 --> 00:08:27,315
And Rawatbhata, where this story began, got its own.

161
00:08:27,315 --> 00:08:34,141
Unit seven joined the grid in March twenty twenty five, and was running at full power by February twenty twenty six.

162
00:08:34,141 --> 00:08:41,373
And this September, on the same lake you saw at the start, where Canada walked out, unit eight began taking in its fuel.

163
00:08:41,373 --> 00:08:46,605
About four thousand seven hundred bundles, for three hundred and ninety two tubes.

164
00:08:46,605 --> 00:08:49,120
Now the honest part, which you deserve.

165
00:08:49,120 --> 00:08:56,379
After all these years, out of every hundred units of electricity India makes, only about three come from nuclear.

166
00:08:56,379 --> 00:08:58,546
And were the new units on time?

167
00:08:58,546 --> 00:08:59,183
No.

168
00:08:59,183 --> 00:09:02,540
Kakrapar's first concrete was poured in twenty ten.

169
00:09:02,540 --> 00:09:04,737
It went critical ten years later.

170
00:09:04,737 --> 00:09:08,603
You may have read that the government has approved ten more, as one fleet.

171
00:09:08,603 --> 00:09:13,428
It said about seventy thousand crore rupees of orders would go to Indian industry.

172
00:09:13,428 --> 00:09:16,569
And here is the quiet ending to the old fight.

173
00:09:16,569 --> 00:09:23,479
Earlier this year, a Canadian company signed to sell India nearly twenty two million pounds of uranium concentrate.

174
00:09:23,479 --> 00:09:28,211
That is about ten thousand tonnes, from twenty twenty seven to twenty thirty five.

175
00:09:28,211 --> 00:09:29,250
Not revenge.

176
00:09:29,250 --> 00:09:31,262
Two countries, trading again.

177
00:09:31,262 --> 00:09:32,779
So what does it tell you?

178
00:09:32,779 --> 00:09:34,979
The fuel, India can buy.

179
00:09:34,979 --> 00:09:38,636
The skill to open its own reactor, nobody could sell.

180
00:09:38,636 --> 00:09:40,407
India had to grow it.

181
00:09:40,407 --> 00:09:42,231
So here is your date to watch.

182
00:09:42,231 --> 00:09:45,502
The day Rajasthan unit eight goes critical.

183
00:09:45,502 --> 00:09:49,369
When it does, it will stand on the same ground Canada left.

184
00:09:49,369 --> 00:09:53,919
A machine is truly yours only when you can open it, and mend it.

185
00:09:53,919 --> 00:10:00,423
Next, I will take you to seven in the evening, when India's lights come on and the grid holds its breath.

186
00:10:00,423 --> 00:10:02,552
Subscribe, so you are there with me.

187
00:10:02,552 --> 00:10:03,930
And tell me in the comments.

188
00:10:03,930 --> 00:10:07,118
Would you have waited for help, or opened it yourself?

189
00:10:07,118 --> 00:10:09,114
This is Be Practical with Kishore.

190
00:10:09,114 --> 00:10:09,990
Stay practical.

